---
/**
 * /tools/tender-compliance-matrix
 *
 * THE SITE'S ONLY FREE TOOL, as of 2026-08-04, and the first that is a SAMPLE
 * OF THE PRODUCT rather than a satellite of it. It shipped as the second, beside
 * the PPN 002 calculator, which the owner removed the same day on the ground
 * that it was not giving any value: that tool did arithmetic a bidder could do
 * unaided, while this reads a document and returns what it says, which is the
 * thing CrowMark is for. Everything it emits is shaped as the platform's own
 * `ExportRow`, so the upgrade path is an import rather than a translation. See
 * the header of lib/tender-matrix.ts.
 *
 * ── BUILT ON THE PPN 002 PAGE'S SHAPE, AND ON ITS THREE PAID-FOR BUGS ───────
 *
 * That page is deleted, so this comment is now the only record of the three
 * defects it cost a session each to find. All three apply here and all three are
 * answered, which is the reason to keep the record rather than the page:
 *
 *   1. THE RESULT RENDERED OFF-SCREEN. The port of the calculator dropped the
 *      scroll-into-view the legacy engine had, so pressing the button changed
 *      nothing a reader could see and the owner reported the tool as "not
 *      working". `reveal()` below is that fix, with the same measured 73px
 *      header threshold and the same two-frame wait. It was a SECOND COPY while
 *      the calculator was frozen by owner directive and could not be refactored
 *      to share a helper. The freeze ended in a deletion rather than a thaw, so
 *      there is no second copy any more: this is the only one, and it is the
 *      module a third tool would share.
 *
 *   2. ONE ERROR SENTENCE FOR EVERY KIND OF MISTAKE. Per-field problems come
 *      from `problems()` in lib/tender-matrix.ts, with `aria-invalid` set and
 *      focus moved. There is one field here so there is one message, but the
 *      MECHANISM is the calculator's and a second field inherits it.
 *
 *   3. THE SELECT OPENED WHITE ON WHITE. Not reachable: there is no select on
 *      this page. `.field__input` comes from styles/forms.css unchanged either
 *      way, which is the point of that file.
 *
 * IT ALSO INHERITS THE CALCULATOR'S DELIBERATE OMISSION. No soft-wall gate, no
 * run counter, no email capture. This page says "no account, no email gate", and
 * a gate that can interrupt the result would contradict its own copy. If a gate
 * is ever wanted, the copy has to change with it.
 *
 * ── EVERY SENTENCE OF THE RESULT IS IN THIS MARKUP, NOT IN THE SCRIPT ───────
 *
 * The result panel below is rendered in full, hidden, and the script fills five
 * numbers into it and toggles four `hidden` attributes. Nothing it says is
 * written in JavaScript. Three reasons, in the order they mattered:
 *
 *   MEASURED. The first build of this page assembled the copy in the script and
 *   compiled to an 8,615 B chunk. scripts/check-budgets.js caps ALL JavaScript
 *   in the build at 15 KB (A-73, owner decision, ADR 0010), and the site was at
 *   12,231 B, so the headroom was 3,129 B. Copy in a script is paid for in the
 *   tightest budget on the site; the same copy in markup is paid for in the
 *   per-route HTML budget, which had 55 KB spare on this route.
 *
 *   IT IS CONTENT. Result copy is editorial, and editorial belongs where the
 *   rest of the page's editorial is, reviewable in the template rather than
 *   inside a submit handler.
 *
 *   IT IS READABLE WITHOUT SCRIPT running, so a crawler and a reader with
 *   JavaScript off both see what the tool reports rather than an empty div.
 *
 * ── WHAT IS NOT IMPORTED, AND WHY THAT IS CORRECT ──────────────────────────
 *
 * styles/forms.css is NOT imported here. layouts/Base.astro imports it for the
 * whole site, and the note beside that import says why: it used to be imported
 * per form COMPONENT, so it was "available to exactly the components that import
 * it", and every page that wanted a field and did not know to import it
 * hand-copied the control instead. Importing it again would be harmless to the
 * bundle and wrong about the architecture. Base owns it.
 *
 * ── IT MUST NEVER ASSERT COMPLIANCE ────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Whether a bid or a procurement is compliant is a judgement for the bidder and
 * the contracting authority. This page reports what its rules matched and stops.
 * Nothing on it scores, rates, ranks or grades anything, and there is no claim
 * about win rates here or anywhere else on this site. Same rule as
 * lib/ppn002.ts, and the third section states it to the reader in those words
 * rather than only in a comment.
 */
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../../../components/layout/Section.astro';
import Button from '../../../components/ui/Button.astro';
import FinalCta from '../../../components/sections/FinalCta.astro';
import { breadcrumbs } from '../../../lib/schema';
import { SITE } from '../../../data/site';
import { MAX_CHARS, MAX_ROWS } from '../../../lib/tender-matrix';

const title = 'Tender Compliance Matrix | Free, no account | CrowAgent';
const description =
  'Free tender compliance matrix for UK bidders. Paste tender text and get the requirements the wording states, each with the line it was read from. Rule based, no upload, no account.';

/**
 * The nine rules, written for a reader rather than as regular expressions.
 *
 * PUBLISHED IN FULL, DELIBERATELY. A tool that reports what a document says has
 * to be checkable, and the only way to check a rule engine is to know what the
 * rules are. Same argument the calculator's traceability ledger makes for its
 * proxy values, one object along: a figure whose provenance is hidden is a
 * figure a bidder cannot defend to a buyer.
 */
const RULES = [
  {
    term: 'Obligation wording',
    detail:
      'Lines containing "must", "shall", "is or are required to", "is or are expected to", "is or are obliged to", "will be required", "will be expected", "undertakes to", "agrees to", "warrants that", "responsible for", "is or are not permitted", "is or are prohibited from", "on no account", "it is a condition", "assumes liability or responsibility", or "indemnifies". A trigger anywhere in the line counts, so a clause that separates it from its verb with a long aside, as in "the Bidder is, subject to Schedule B, entirely responsible for licensing", is still read. Prohibitions count: "must not" is an obligation, and reading it as anything else would report the opposite of what the document says.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Numbered questions',
    detail:
      'A numbered line that opens with an instruction, such as "Q4 Provide evidence of public liability insurance" or "3.6 Demonstrate how you will meet the requirement". A question schedule states its requirements as instructions rather than with the word "must", so a rule that reads only obligation wording skips the part of the pack you actually have to answer. The leading number is required: an instruction without one is treated as prose, because the numbering is the evidence that it is a question rather than a sentence.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Numbered references',
    detail:
      'Leading question and clause numbers such as 1.1, 4.2.3, Q1 and SQ2, part-lettered and roman numbering such as A.1.1, B.1.1.ii, C.1.2 and IV.2.b(iii), lettered sub-clauses such as 3.4.a and 3.2.1.b.iv, and stacked parentheses such as A.1.3(a), 1.01.01(A) and 1.1.1(a)(iv)(A). A leading section mark or bullet is stepped over, so "§ 2.1.3" and "- 5.1.a" are read as 2.1.3 and 5.1.a. A numbered line on its own is a heading rather than a requirement, so it earns a row only when the line also states an obligation, an instruction, a limit or a weighting. A number that is not a clause is not read as one: a figure written 2025.10 stays a figure, and a line opening "500 words" is not a reference to clause 500.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Pass or fail wording',
    detail:
      'Lines that mark themselves as decisive: "pass/fail", "is mandatory", "a mandatory requirement", "a minimum requirement", "fail or failure to comply, provide, submit, meet or respond", "will be rejected, excluded or disqualified", "will not be accepted", "will not proceed", "will result in disqualification, exclusion or rejection", "deemed non-compliant", "shall be excluded", "grounds for exclusion", "strictly prohibited". The rule reads the marker the document wrote. It does not judge how serious anything is, and it does not mean the rows without it are safe.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Closing dates and times',
    detail:
      'A date or a time on a line that also says it is a closing point: "deadline", "closing date or time", "must be received, submitted, returned or uploaded", "no later than", "due by or on", "returned by", "expires at or on", "cut-off". The cue is required because a pack is full of dates that are not deadlines, such as a contract start or a reporting period. "By" and "before" also count as cues, but only when a number follows them, and only a real date or time found nearby produces a row, so "increased by 20%" states no deadline. Both date orders are read, "14 March 2026" and "September 30, 2026", along with clock times to the second. Nothing is converted into a calendar date: "14/03/2026" and "3 February" are shown exactly as the document wrote them, because guessing a year or a date order would put a figure of our invention next to a real quote.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Response limits',
    detail:
      'Word, page, character and side counts, written any of the three ways a tender pack writes them: "no more than 500 words", "500 word limit", "1,000 characters or fewer".',
  },
  {
    term: 'Percentage weightings',
    detail:
      'Percentages within three lines of a mention of weighting, evaluation, scoring, criteria, marks or award, where the line itself either reads as a criteria row ("Quality 60%") or mentions evaluation in its own right ("quality will be weighted at 60%"). Both tests are needed: nearness alone reads a VAT rate or a retention percentage sitting under an award criteria heading as though it were a criterion. A criteria row is a short label and a number, so "Quality 60%" and "Quality 60%, Price 30%, Social Value 10%" are read and "Payment terms allow a 2% early settlement discount and VAT is charged at 20%" is not. A percentage in brackets, as in "Technical Merit (60%)", is allowed a longer label, because a bracketed figure is an aside attached to the phrase before it, which is what a weighting is. The tool adds up what it found and shows the total. A pack listing sub-criteria as well as top-level criteria sums to well over 100, which is normal and is not a fault in the document.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Quoted wording',
    detail:
      'A trigger word that appears only inside double quotation marks is read as the document naming a phrase rather than using it, and does not make the line a requirement. A definitions section reading: the phrase "must comply" indicates a mandatory requirement, is describing wording rather than stating an obligation. If the same or another trigger also appears outside the quotation marks, the line is a requirement as normal, so a clause reading: the Bidder must state "Compliant" or "Non-Compliant", keeps its obligation. Only trigger words are treated this way. Figures are not: a limit written inside quotation marks is still a limit the document states. A line that both quotes a phrase and defines one, as in: the phrase "is strictly prohibited" denotes a zero-tolerance policy, is read as a glossary entry and produces no row at all. Both halves are required, so a clause that merely contains quotation marks keeps its requirement.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Wrapped lines',
    detail:
      'Text copied from a PDF breaks wherever the column ended, not where the sentence did. A line that does not end in punctuation and is followed by a line starting in lower case is read as one statement, because English does not begin a sentence with a lower case letter. Without this a limit written "no more than 2,000" on one line and "words" on the next is not found at all.',
  },
];

/**
 * THE LIMITS SECTION IS NOT SMALL PRINT. It is the reason this tool can be
 * published at all. Every line is a statement about what the tool does, which is
 * the only kind of claim this page is in a position to make.
 */
const LIMITS = [
  'It does not assert compliance. Whether a bid or a procurement is compliant is a judgement for the bidder and the contracting authority. This lists what the wording states and stops.',
  'It will miss requirements. A rule that keys on the word "must" cannot see a requirement written as "the successful supplier provides monthly reporting". Silence from these rules is not evidence that a document requires nothing.',
  'It does not paraphrase. Every row carries the segment it was read from, word for word. A full extractor restates a requirement in a normalised form and reads better for it; a rule engine doing the same would be inventing, because it has nothing to restate from.',
  'It does not read files. Paste text. There is no upload in this version, so no document leaves your machine.',
  'It does not score, rank or rate anything, and it makes no claim about win rates.',
  'It sends nothing anywhere. The text is read in your browser. There is no account, no email gate and no request to any server.',
];

const schema = [
  /*
   * WebApplication, matching the calculator's node field for field.
   * `isAccessibleForFree` and the zero-price Offer are what make "free, no
   * account" machine-readable, which is this page's whole claim.
   */
  {
    '@type': 'WebApplication',
    name: 'Tender Compliance Matrix',
    applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Web',
    url: `${SITE.origin}/tools/tender-compliance-matrix`,
    description,
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'GBP' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'CrowAgent', url: `${SITE.origin}/` },
  },
  breadcrumbs([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Tools', path: '/tools' },
    { name: 'Tender Compliance Matrix', path: '/tools/tender-compliance-matrix' },
  ]),
];
---

<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/tools/tender-compliance-matrix"
  schema={schema}
>
  <Section
    as="h1"
    eyebrow="Free, no account"
    title="Tender Compliance Matrix"
    standfirst="Paste the text of a tender, a question schedule or an evaluation section. The tool lists the requirements the wording states, each with the line it was read from. Nothing is uploaded, nothing is sent anywhere, and there is no sign-up."
    id="matrix"
  >
    <form class="form matrix-form" id="matrix-form" novalidate>
      <div class="field">
        <label class="field__label" for="tenderText">
          Tender text
          <span class="field__req" aria-hidden="true">*</span>
          <span class="visually-hidden">(required)</span>
        </label>
        <textarea
          class="field__input field__input--area matrix-form__area"
          id="tenderText"
          name="tenderText"
          rows="12"
          placeholder="Paste a question schedule, an evaluation section or a specification extract."
          required
          aria-describedby="tenderText-help matrix-err"></textarea>
        <p class="field__help" id="tenderText-help">
          Up to {MAX_CHARS.toLocaleString('en-GB')} characters at a time. The text is read in this
          browser and is never sent to a server.
        </p>

        {/*
          An error interrupts; a result does not. Two regions, two roles.

          THE ALERT REGION IS ALWAYS PRESENT AND ITS SENTENCES ARE HIDDEN, for
          the same two reasons as the result panel: a live region has to be in
          the accessibility tree before the change it announces, and the copy
          belongs in the markup rather than in the script. `problems()` in
          lib/tender-matrix.ts returns a CODE and this is where each code's
          sentence is written, once.

          IT SITS INSIDE `.field`, NOT AFTER THE BUTTON ROW, and the reason is
          the one above. The calculator can put its message after its buttons
          because that element is `hidden` when there is nothing to say, so it
          costs no space; this one can never be hidden, and `.form` is a flex
          column with a `--stack-2` gap, so an always-present region below the
          buttons spends 18 to 26px of empty page on every visit. Inside the
          field it costs the field's own 6px, and it is beside the control it is
          about, which is where WCAG 3.3.1 wants it anyway.
        */}
        <div class="field__err" id="matrix-err" role="alert">
          <p id="e-empty" hidden>
            Paste the tender text you want read. Nothing is sent anywhere: the text stays in this
            browser.
          </p>
          <p id="e-short" hidden>
            That is too short to read as tender text. Paste at least a paragraph, such as a
            question schedule or an evaluation section.
          </p>
          <p id="e-long" hidden>
            That is longer than this tool reads in one pass. Paste a section at a time.
          </p>
          {/* Unreachable while `analyse()` refuses exactly what `problems()`
              reports, which it does by calling it. Written down rather than left
              to a silent `return`, because a control that does nothing and says
              nothing is the defect this page was built to avoid. */}
          <p id="e-unread" hidden>That text could not be read. Check it and try again.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="btn-row">
        <Button type="submit">Build the matrix</Button>
        <Button type="button" variant="secondary" id="btn-sample">Load a sample</Button>
        <Button type="reset" variant="secondary">Clear</Button>
      </div>

      {/*
        ── THE SAMPLE IS THE FIRST THING MOST VISITORS CAN ACTUALLY DO ─────────
        A tool whose only input is pasted tender text asks a first-time reader to
        go and find a tender pack before it will show them anything. Most
        arrivals have no ITT open, so the honest default state of this page is a
        blank box and a button that does nothing for them.

        THE TEXT LIVES IN THE MARKUP, not in the script, for the reason recorded
        at the head of this file: the JavaScript budget is the tightest on the
        site and this extract is roughly 900 bytes of copy. In a <template> it is
        paid for in the per-route HTML budget, which has room, and it is inert
        until the button is pressed.

        IT IS LABELLED AS WRITTEN FOR THE PURPOSE, and that matters on a page
        whose whole claim is that it never invents. It is not a real tender and
        does not pretend to be one; it is written to contain one of each thing
        the rules below look for, so that what comes back can be checked against
        the rules as published.
      */}
      <p class="matrix-form__sample">
        The sample is a short worked example written for this page. It is not a real
        tender, and it contains one of each thing the rules below look for, including
        two percentages that are deliberately not weightings.
      </p>

      {/* Same reasoning as the calculator's: this form has no action and reads
          the text entirely in the browser, so with scripts off pressing the
          button reloads the page with the box wiped and nothing to show for it.
          It carries no spam check because nothing is submitted anywhere, which
          is the same fact that lets the tool ask for no sign-up. */}
      <noscript>
        <p class="form__noscript">
          This tool reads the text in your browser, so it needs JavaScript. Nothing is sent to a
          server either way.
        </p>
      </noscript>
    </form>

    {/*
      THE LIVE REGION IS THE WRAPPER AND IT IS NEVER HIDDEN. A live region has to
      be in the accessibility tree BEFORE the content lands in it, or the change
      it exists to announce happened while it was not there. The calculator
      solves this by unhiding an EMPTY panel and then filling it; this panel is
      not empty when hidden, because all of its copy is pre-rendered, so the same
      trick would flash the previous run's numbers. An always-present wrapper
      with the card hidden inside it gets the announcement right without showing
      an empty card on load.

      ONLY THE SUMMARY SPEAKS. `role="status"` announces its whole subtree, so
      putting three hundred rows of tender text inside it would read the document
      aloud on every submit. The summary IS the answer and it is short. The
      matrix follows it in document order and the summary says so.
    */}
    <div role="status" aria-live="polite">
      <div id="matrix-result" class="result surface surface--pad" hidden>
        <p class="meta result__kicker">What these rules matched</p>

        <p class="result__lead" id="r-found" hidden>
          Requirements matched: <b id="r-total">0</b>. Each row below carries the line it was read
          from, word for word. Nothing here states whether a bid or a procurement is compliant.
        </p>
        <p class="result__lead" id="r-none" hidden>
          These rules matched no requirements in that text. That does not mean the document states
          none: the rules key on wording, and a requirement written without it is invisible to them.
        </p>

        <dl class="deflist deflist--ledger">
          <dt>Lines read</dt>
          <dd id="r-lines">0</dd>
          <dt>Obligations found</dt>
          <dd id="r-obligations">0</dd>
          <dt>Numbered questions found</dt>
          <dd id="r-directives">0</dd>
          <dt>Clause references found</dt>
          <dd id="r-references">0</dd>
          <dt>Marked pass or fail</dt>
          <dd id="r-mandatory">0</dd>
          <dt>Dates and times stated</dt>
          <dd id="r-deadlines">0</dd>
          <dt>Response limits found</dt>
          <dd id="r-limits">0</dd>
          <dt>Percentage weightings found</dt>
          <dd id="r-wcount">0</dd>
          <dt>Those weightings total</dt>
          <dd id="r-wtotal">0%</dd>
        </dl>

        <p class="result__note" id="r-wcomplete" hidden>
          Percentage weightings appear to sum to a complete set (100%).
        </p>
        <p class="result__note" id="r-wnote" hidden>
          The total is the arithmetic sum of every percentage found on a line that also reads as
          evaluation criteria. A pack that lists sub-criteria alongside top-level criteria sums to
          more than 100, and the tool makes no judgement about whether a set of weightings is right.
        </p>
        <p class="result__note" id="r-wnocontext" hidden>
          Percentages were found in the text but no evaluation context was detected. If this is an award criteria section, include its section heading.
        </p>
        <p class="result__note" id="r-dupe" hidden>
          Wording that appeared more than once is listed once. Duplicate lines dropped:
          <b id="r-dupes">0</b>.
        </p>
        <p class="result__note" id="r-trunc" hidden>
          The first {MAX_ROWS} are listed below. Paste a section at a time to read the rest.
        </p>
        <div class="result__actions btn-row" id="r-actions" hidden>
          <button type="button" class="btn btn--secondary btn--sm" id="btn-csv">Download CSV</button>
          <button type="button" class="btn btn--secondary btn--sm" id="btn-copy">Copy Markdown</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <ol
      id="matrix-rows"
      class="matrix"
      role="list"
      aria-label="Requirements matched in the pasted text"
      hidden
    >
    </ol>

    {/* One row, authored in markup and cloned per result. The structure and the
        class names stay in the template where they can be read, and the script
        does nothing but put text into four places and add chips. */}
    {/* Read by the "Load a sample extract" button. Inert until then. */}
    <template id="sample-text">INVITATION TO TENDER - GROUNDS MAINTENANCE SERVICES

Section 1: Instructions to Tenderers

1.1 Tenderers must submit their response via the procurement portal.
1.2 Tenders must be received by 12:00 noon on 14 March 2026. Late submissions will be rejected.
1.3 Tenderers are required to provide a written methodology of no more than 2,000 words.

Section 2: Selection Questions

Q1 Provide evidence of employers liability insurance of at least 5 million pounds.
Q2 Confirm your organisation holds a current Carbon Reduction Plan. This is a pass/fail requirement.
Q3 The bidder shall demonstrate a minimum of three years relevant experience.

Section 3: Award Criteria

The contract will be awarded on the basis of the most advantageous tender.

Quality 60%
Social Value 10%
Price 30%

Payment terms allow a 2% early settlement discount and VAT is charged at 20%.</template>

    <template id="matrix-row">
      <li class="matrix__row">
        <p class="meta matrix__ref">
          <span class="matrix__no"></span><span class="matrix__line"></span>
        </p>
        <div class="matrix__main">
          <p class="matrix__req"></p>
          <p class="matrix__quote"><q></q></p>
        </div>
        <p class="matrix__tags"></p>
      </li>
    </template>

    <p class="basis">
      The rules are published in full below. They read wording, not meaning, so a requirement
      written without obligation language, a number or a reference is invisible to them. This tool
      records what a document says. It does not decide whether anything is compliant, and it is not
      legal or procurement advice.
    </p>

    {/* RE-POINTED 2026-08-04. This recommended the free PPN 002 calculator,
        which the owner removed the same day. The sentence is kept rather than
        deleted because the QUESTION a reader arrives with is real: this tool
        reads what a tender says, and a reader who wants the social value rule
        itself needs somewhere to go. That somewhere is the glossary entry, which
        states the 10% minimum and the dates it binds from, and it is also where
        both removed URLs now 301. */}
    <p class="more">
      Looking for the social value rule itself?
      <a href="/glossary/ppn-002">Read PPN 002 in full</a>, including the mandatory 10% minimum
      social-value weighting and the dates it applies from.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    eyebrow="Published in full"
    title="What the rules look for."
    standfirst="Four rules, all deterministic. There is no model, no network call and nothing guessed. Every row the tool produces can be traced back to the characters that triggered it."
    id="rules"
  >
    <dl class="deflist">
      {
        RULES.map((r) => (
          <>
            <dt>{r.term}</dt>
            <dd>{r.detail}</dd>
          </>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
  </Section>

  <Section
    eyebrow="Stated plainly"
    title="What this tool does not do."
    standfirst="Written down because a free tool that overstates itself costs more credibility than it earns attention."
    id="limits"
  >
    <ul class="notes">
      {LIMITS.map((l) => <li>{l}</li>)}
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <FinalCta
    eyebrow="Go further"
    title="Need the whole bid read, not one section?"
    standfirst="CrowMark reads a full tender pack, drafts answers from your own submitted bids with the source beside each one, and tracks what you promised after the contract is awarded."
    id="product"
    links={[
      { label: 'All free tools', href: '/tools' },
      { label: 'PPN 002 explained', href: '/glossary/ppn-002' },
    ]}
  >
    <Button href="/contact?enquiry=limited-access#contact-form">Request access</Button>
    <Button href="/crowmark" variant="secondary">Explore CrowMark</Button>
  </FinalCta>
</Base>

<script>
  import {
    analyse,
    problems,
    pct,
    num,
    buildCsv,
    buildMarkdown,
    exportFilename,
  } from '../../../lib/tender-matrix';
  import type { MatrixRow, TenderMatrix } from '../../../lib/tender-matrix';

  /*
   * Read once, the same way scripts/motion.ts and the calculator read it. A user
   * who changes the setting mid-visit gets the new behaviour on reload; a user
   * who has it set never sees this page move under them.
   */
  const REDUCED =
    typeof window.matchMedia === 'function' &&
    window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

  function initMatrix() {
    const byId = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id);

    const form = byId('matrix-form') as HTMLFormElement | null;
    const input = byId('tenderText') as HTMLTextAreaElement | null;
    const panel = byId('matrix-result');
    const list = byId('matrix-rows');
    const err = byId('matrix-err');
    const tpl = byId('matrix-row') as HTMLTemplateElement | null;
    if (!form || !input || !panel || !list || !err || !tpl) return;

    const put = (id: string, text: string) => {
      const node = byId(id);
      if (node) node.textContent = text;
    };

    const show = (id: string, on: boolean) => {
      const node = byId(id);
      if (node) node.hidden = !on;
    };

    /*
     * THE MEASURED SCROLL FIX, carried over from the PPN 002 calculator page
     * before it was deleted. The geometry it recorded: at 1440x900 the button
     * sits above the fold, the result lands below it, and without this the page
     * does not move, so pressing the button looks like pressing a dead control.
     *
     * IT SCROLLS ONLY WHEN IT HAS TO, so running the tool twice with the panel
     * already on screen does not yank the page.
     *
     * THE THRESHOLD IS THE HEADER, NOT THE SCROLL PADDING. <html> carries
     * scroll-padding-top: 96px, but a settled panel MEASURES at 76, because the
     * section's arrival transform is still resolving when the scroll is issued.
     * The sticky header is 73px, so 76 clears it. Testing against 96 would call
     * a visible panel hidden and re-scroll on every submit.
     *
     * TWO FRAMES, NOT ONE. The element is unhidden immediately before this runs
     * and its box is not final until the frame after the one that lays it out.
     */
    const HEADER = 73;
    const reveal = (el: HTMLElement) =>
      requestAnimationFrame(() =>
        requestAnimationFrame(() => {
          const r = el.getBoundingClientRect();
          if (r.top >= HEADER && r.bottom <= window.innerHeight) return;
          el.scrollIntoView({ behavior: REDUCED ? 'auto' : 'smooth', block: 'start' });
        })
      );

    /* Every sentence hidden, not just the one that was shown. `role="alert"`
       announces a CHANGE, so a sentence left in the tree means the same mistake
       made twice in a row is silent the second time.

       IT ASKS THE REGION WHAT IT HOLDS rather than carrying a list of codes. A
       list here would be a second place to remember, and the failure it allows
       is silent: a code added to lib/tender-matrix.ts and not added here leaves
       its sentence on screen under the next answer. */
    const clearError = () => {
      for (const p of err.querySelectorAll('p')) p.hidden = true;
      input.removeAttribute('aria-invalid');
    };

    /* THE LAST MATRIX ANALYSED, WHICH IS WHAT THE TWO EXPORT BUTTONS EXPORT.
       Declared beside the thing that clears it rather than beside the buttons
       that read it, because forgetting to clear it is the failure that matters:
       an export button that hands over the PREVIOUS document's requirements is
       worse than one that does nothing. Null until the first successful parse,
       which is why both buttons check it. */
    let currentMatrix: TenderMatrix | null = null;

    const clearResult = () => {
      panel.hidden = true;
      list.hidden = true;
      list.replaceChildren();
      currentMatrix = null;
      /* THE CLIPBOARD FALLBACK IS A SIBLING OF THE PANEL, NOT A CHILD OF IT, so
         hiding the panel leaves it on screen holding the whole of the previous
         matrix. That is the exact failure the note on the reset handler below
         describes: a reset that leaves the answer up under an empty box reads as
         the tool having ignored the reset. */
      byId('copy-fallback')?.remove();
    };

    /** `obligation` reads as "Obligation". Derived rather than tabulated: a map
        of the five signal names would be script saying what the union members
        already say. */
    const label = (s: string) => s.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + s.slice(1);

    const rowOf = (r: MatrixRow) => {
      const li = (tpl.content.firstElementChild as HTMLElement).cloneNode(true) as HTMLElement;
      const at = (sel: string) => li.querySelector(sel) as HTMLElement;

      /* The reference column carries the document's own reference when the line
         has one, and the line number either way, so a reader can find the row in
         their own paste. Both are facts read from the input. */
      at('.matrix__no').textContent = r.requirement_ref ?? '';
      at('.matrix__line').textContent = `Line ${num(r.source_line)}`;
      at('.matrix__req').textContent = r.requirement_text;
      /* THE EVIDENCE, AND IT IS THE POINT OF THE ROW. `<q>` rather than a styled
         paragraph: the quotation marks come from the user agent, so the fact
         that this is quoted material is carried by the markup rather than by a
         colour. A <blockquote> would be more emphatic and would pull in the
         panelled card recipe in styles/blocks.css, which is right for one
         citation in an article and unreadable stacked three hundred deep. */
      at('q').textContent = r.source_quote ?? '';

      /*
       * Chips: what was seen in the line, and nothing about what it means.
       *
       * NO MARK TOKEN IS USED ON ANY OF THEM, and that is a decision rather than
       * an omission. --c-verified, --c-refused and --c-at-risk mean verified,
       * refused and at risk; a signal is none of those, and painting one teal
       * would assert that something had been checked. Every chip inherits its
       * colour, which is what styles/labels.css asks a call site to do when it
       * has no meaning to add.
       *
       * There is no category chip. See the note above `CATEGORY` in
       * lib/tender-matrix.ts: a keyword classifier is the one part of this tool
       * that would state a conclusion the text does not contain.
       */
      const tags = at('.matrix__tags');
      const chip = (text: string) => {
        const span = document.createElement('span');
        span.className = 'chip';
        span.textContent = text;
        tags.append(span);
      };

      for (const signal of r.signals) {
        /* Both of these are redundant with the value chips below them: the
           limit signal with "500 words", the deadline signal with the date
           itself. A chip reading "Deadline" beside a chip reading "14 March
           2026" says the same thing twice and crowds the row. */
        if (signal === 'limit' || signal === 'deadline') continue;
        chip(
          signal === 'weighting' && r.weight !== null
            ? `Weighting ${pct(r.weight)}`
            : label(signal)
        );
      }
      for (const limit of r.limits) chip(`${num(limit.value)} ${limit.unit}`);
      for (const when of r.deadlines) chip(when.text);

      return li;
    };

    form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
      e.preventDefault();
      clearError();

      /*
       * The offending field is NAMED, marked and focused. `.field__input
       * [aria-invalid='true']` has been in styles/forms.css since the contact
       * form shipped. Colour is the second signal; the message is the first.
       */
      const bad = problems(input.value);
      if (bad.length > 0) {
        clearResult();
        input.setAttribute('aria-invalid', 'true');
        show(`e-${bad[0].code}`, true);
        /* WCAG 3.3.1: put the user at the thing they have to change. */
        input.focus();
        return;
      }

      const m = analyse(input.value);
      /* Unreachable while `analyse()` refuses exactly what `problems()` reports,
         which it does by calling it. Stated rather than assumed, because the one
         thing this page must never do is render a matrix built from nothing. */
      if (!m) {
        clearResult();
        show('e-unread', true);
        return;
      }

      put('r-total', num(m.summary.total));
      put('r-lines', num(m.lines));
      put('r-obligations', num(m.obligationCount));
      put('r-directives', num(m.directiveCount));
      put('r-references', num(m.referenceCount));
      put('r-mandatory', num(m.mandatoryCount));
      put('r-deadlines', num(m.deadlineCount));
      put('r-limits', num(m.limitCount));
      put('r-wcount', num(m.weightingCount));
      put('r-wtotal', pct(m.weightingTotal));
      put('r-dupes', num(m.duplicates));
      show('r-found', m.summary.total > 0);
      show('r-none', m.summary.total === 0);
      show('r-wcomplete', m.weightingConfidence === 'complete');
      show('r-wnote', m.weightingConfidence === 'sub_criteria');
      show('r-wnocontext', m.weightingConfidence === 'no_context_found');
      show('r-dupe', m.duplicates > 0);
      show('r-trunc', m.truncated);
      show('r-actions', m.rows.length > 0);

      currentMatrix = m;
      /* A SECOND ANALYSIS MUST NOT LEAVE THE FIRST ONE'S EXPORT BELOW IT. The
         clipboard fallback is a sibling of the panel, so nothing that repaints
         the panel touches it, and analysing a second document would put the new
         matrix directly above a textarea holding the old one's requirements. */
      byId('copy-fallback')?.remove();

      const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
      for (const r of m.rows) frag.append(rowOf(r));
      list.replaceChildren(frag);
      list.hidden = m.rows.length === 0;

      panel.hidden = false;
      reveal(panel);
    });

    /*
     * THE SAMPLE LOADER. It fills the box and submits, rather than filling the
     * box and leaving the reader to find the button: the whole point is to show
     * someone with no tender pack to hand what the tool returns, and a second
     * required click is where that intent gets lost.
     *
     * `requestSubmit` and NOT `submit`, which is the difference between the two
     * that matters here. `form.submit()` bypasses submit event handlers
     * entirely, so the page would reload and show nothing; `requestSubmit()`
     * fires the handler above exactly as pressing the button does.
     */
    const btnSample = byId('btn-sample');
    const sampleTpl = byId('sample-text') as HTMLTemplateElement | null;
    if (btnSample && sampleTpl) {
      btnSample.addEventListener('click', () => {
        /* `.content.textContent`, NOT `.innerHTML`. The template holds plain
           text, so the parsed fragment already carries it decoded; reading the
           markup instead would mean hand-unescaping &amp;, &lt; and &gt;, which
           is three entities today and a bug the first time the sample gains a
           fourth. It also keeps the whole tool free of any innerHTML read. */
        input.value = (sampleTpl.content.textContent || '').trim();
        form.requestSubmit();
      });
    }

    const btnCsv = byId('btn-csv');
    const btnCopy = byId('btn-copy');

    /*
     * WHAT THE PAGE STILL OWNS, AND IT IS ONLY THE BROWSER PARTS. `buildCsv` and
     * `buildMarkdown` decide what a row means once it leaves the page, so they
     * live in lib/tender-matrix.ts where a fixture can drive them; the Blob, the
     * object URL and the clipboard call are browser plumbing and cannot. That
     * split is also what took this island back under the JavaScript budget: the
     * two exceptions opened for it on 2026-08-06 are deleted rather than raised.
     */
    if (btnCsv) {
      btnCsv.addEventListener('click', () => {
        if (!currentMatrix || !currentMatrix.rows.length) return;
        const blob = new Blob([buildCsv(currentMatrix)], { type: 'text/csv;charset=utf-8;' });
        const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
        const a = document.createElement('a');
        a.href = url;
        a.download = exportFilename(new Date());
        document.body.append(a);
        a.click();
        a.remove();
        /* REVOKED ON THE NEXT TICK, NOT ON THIS ONE. WebKit reads the object URL
           after the click handler returns, so revoking synchronously cancels the
           download on Safari while working everywhere else, which is the kind of
           fault that only ever shows up on somebody else's machine. */
        setTimeout(() => URL.revokeObjectURL(url), 0);
      });
    }

    if (btnCopy) {
      btnCopy.addEventListener('click', async () => {
        if (!currentMatrix || !currentMatrix.rows.length) return;
        const markdown = buildMarkdown(currentMatrix);
        const said = btnCopy.textContent;
        try {
          await navigator.clipboard.writeText(markdown);
          btnCopy.textContent = 'Copied';
          setTimeout(() => {
            btnCopy.textContent = said;
          }, 2000);
        } catch {
          /* THE CLIPBOARD IS NOT AVAILABLE ON EVERY ORIGIN, and a button that
             does nothing is worse than no button. navigator.clipboard is absent
             over plain HTTP and can be refused by permission policy, so the
             table goes into a read only textarea the visitor can select and
             copy by hand. An empty catch here would swallow the one failure
             this feature actually has. */
          const existing = byId('copy-fallback');
          if (existing) existing.remove();
          const fb = document.createElement('textarea');
          fb.id = 'copy-fallback';
          fb.className = 'field__input field__input--area';
          fb.rows = 6;
          fb.readOnly = true;
          fb.value = markdown;
          fb.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Markdown table: select all and copy');
          panel.after(fb);
          fb.focus();
          fb.select();
        }
      });
    }

    form.addEventListener('reset', () => {
      /* The browser clears the field; this clears everything the browser does
         not know about. A reset that leaves the matrix on screen under an empty
         box reads as the tool having ignored the reset. */
      clearResult();
      clearError();
    });
  }

  initMatrix();
</script>

<style>
  /* ── THE FORM BLOCK IS CENTRED, LIKE EVERYTHING ABOVE AND BELOW IT ────────
   *
   * Same rule the calculator applies to `.calc`, and the same reason: a capped
   * column in a full-measure container hugs the left edge and reads as a second
   * layout stacked on the first. The FIELDS stay left-aligned, because a form is
   * read and filled in down a left edge. What moves is the block.
   *
   * THE CAP IS A TOKEN, NOT A NUMBER. `.calc` uses `max-width: 560px` and needed
   * an entry in check-design-system.js to say so. --measure-prose is a fraction
   * of --measure, so this column stays in proportion if the measure ever moves,
   * and no allow-list entry is spent. */
  .matrix-form {
    max-width: var(--measure-prose);
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  /* Taller than the shared 140px floor, because the thing being pasted is a
     section of a document rather than a message. `.field__input--area` in
     styles/forms.css owns everything else about the control: the border, the
     radius, the fill, the focus ring and the resize handle. */
  .matrix-form__area {
    min-height: 15rem;
  }

  /* The alert region holds its sentences as paragraphs rather than as one text
     node, so the user agent's paragraph margin has to go. `.field__err` in
     styles/forms.css owns the face, size, weight and colour, all of which these
     inherit; this states nothing about how an error looks. */
  .field__err p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* ── THE SUMMARY PANEL ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * `surface surface--pad` and nothing else. The card, its border, its radius,
   * its padding, its specular highlight and its shadow all come from
   * styles/surfaces.css, and the ledger inside it from styles/blocks.css, which
   * calls that shape what it is: a two-column form for a run of short scalar
   * values. What is declared here is where the block sits and how its own
   * paragraphs are spaced. */
  .result {
    margin-top: var(--sec-gap);
  }

  .result__kicker {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    color: var(--c-ambient-start);
  }

  .result__lead {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-2);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .result__note {
    margin: var(--stack-1) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  /* Export actions row sits below the ledger notes with the same stack gap.
     The pair of buttons share equal width via .btn-row's 1fr grid tracks. */
  .result__actions {
    margin-top: var(--stack-2);
  }

  /* The sentence under the button row explaining what the sample is. Same
     treatment as .field__help, which is the other explanatory line in this
     form, so the two do not read as different kinds of note. */
  .matrix-form__sample {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  /* ── THE MATRIX ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * A RULED REGISTER, NOT THREE HUNDRED CARDS. The obvious build is one
   * `.surface` per row, and it is wrong at this length: styles/surfaces.css says
   * so itself about "a dense stack where two hundred shadows would read as mud".
   * A hairline between rows is what every other register on this site uses and
   * it costs one border declaration.
   *
   * IT IS AN <ol> OF <li>, WHICH IS ALSO WHY ITS ALIGNMENT IS FREE.
   * styles/alignment.css sets `main :is(ul, ol) > li { text-align: left }` for
   * the document, because a list is read down a left edge. No opt-out is spent
   * here, and scripts/check-render.js excuses an LI inside a list by the same
   * rule. `role="list"` is on the element because `list-style: none` removes
   * list semantics in Safari.
   *
   * THE ROW RULES ARE :global BECAUSE THE ROWS ARE CLONED FROM A <template>.
   * Astro scopes a component's selectors by adding a class to the elements it
   * compiles, and whether that reaches inside a <template> is an implementation
   * detail this page should not depend on. Written as `:global` under the
   * scoped `.matrix` ancestor, the rules match a cloned row either way, which
   * is the same pattern the calculator uses for its script-built result card. */
  .matrix {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--sec-gap) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
  }

  .matrix :global(.matrix__row) {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 7rem minmax(0, 1fr) 13rem;
    gap: var(--stack-1) clamp(16px, 2.5vw, 32px);
    padding: var(--stack-2) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--c-border);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .matrix :global(.matrix__row:first-child) {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--c-border);
  }

  /* `.meta` from styles/labels.css carries the face, size, case and tracking.
     Colour is deliberately not part of that recipe, so the call site states it,
     and this one takes the near end of the ambient ramp for the same reason the
     calculator's reference column does: it is provenance, not a verdict. */
  .matrix :global(.matrix__ref) {
    color: var(--c-ambient-start);
  }

  /* Both parts of the reference cell sit on their own line. The spans inherit
     every part of the `.meta` recipe from their parent, so they are the same
     micro-label rather than a fifth one; what is stated here is only that they
     stack and which ink the second one takes. */
  .matrix :global(.matrix__no),
  .matrix :global(.matrix__line) {
    display: block;
  }

  .matrix :global(.matrix__line) {
    margin-top: 4px;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  .matrix :global(.matrix__req) {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  /* The verbatim source, one step back in the ink ladder so the requirement
     reads first and the evidence second, which is the order a reader wants them
     in. The quotation marks come from `<q>` and the user agent, so the fact that
     this is quoted material survives with every pixel of colour removed. */
  .matrix :global(.matrix__quote) {
    margin: var(--stack-1) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  .matrix :global(.matrix__tags) {
    margin: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-content: start;
    gap: 0.4rem;
  }

  /* ── THE TWO CLOSING PARAGRAPHS ───────────────────────────────────────────
     Same treatment the calculator gives its basis line and its onward link. The
     link takes no rule of its own: styles/links.css owns the underline for every
     link inside a paragraph on this site, and a second offset here would be one
     of the seven recipes that file was written to end. */
  .basis,
  .more {
    margin: var(--sec-gap) auto 0;
    max-width: var(--measure-prose);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  .more {
    font-size: var(--t-body);
  }

  /* ── THE LIMITS LIST ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     A plain list of statements. It states its own indent because the user
     agent's 40px is set for a nested outline rather than for a register, and
     nothing else, so it is one page's content layout rather than a treatment
     anything else could want. */
  .notes {
    margin: 0;
    padding-inline-start: 1.2rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--stack-2);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .matrix :global(.matrix__row) {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
